WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:02.630
Siltstone is dominated by silt.

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:05.890
Mud includes both silt and clay, and its rock can be

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:10.830
called mudstone or mudrock. Shale characteristically splits into thin chips or

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.490
flakes. Fine grain size and splitting are separate observations.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:18.670
Interbedded sand or limestone and varying colors add information about a

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:19.650
particular sequence.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:25.230
Floodwaters spread fine sediment beyond a river channel, and slow water

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.490
lets silt settle. Lakes receive mud from streams, wind and nearby

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.430
slopes. Wind can also deposit extensive silt layers, especially from sediment

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:39.410
ground by glaciers. Chemical weathering supplies clay-rich soils that may later

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:40.590
become fine-grained rock.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:46.070
Tidal mud flats are repeatedly flooded and exposed, sometimes leaving evaporite

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:50.250
minerals after drying. Lagoons trap clay and mud delivered from land.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.830
Shallow continental seas and shelves also accumulate fines.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.210
Small grain size therefore supports quiet deposition, but does not by

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.690
itself mean the water was very deep.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:05.170
Fine particles carried by currents and wind can reach ocean basins.

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:09.610
Organic-rich mud in relatively low-oxygen conditions can produce dark shale.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:13.670
The interpretation depends on composition and fossils as well as color.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:17.610
A dark fine-grained rock is evidence to investigate, not a direct

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.930
measurement of an exact ancient water depth.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:25.250
Shale, mudstone and siltstone commonly weather into soft slopes and loose

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.910
chips. Debris from stronger cliffs can cover these weak units.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.830
Streams preferentially erode some weak layers, while intricately drained badlands develop

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:38.250
in easily eroded sediment. Modern slope shape helps identify resistance;

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.390
depositional environment still requires the rock evidence.
